text=f'''The scorecard is built and connected to the peer-selection and loan-valuation calculations as a separate experiment. These are computed results, not forecasts. Production selection, thesis methodology and published tables are unchanged.

Every headline count below refers to the **same 778 low-/middle-income country-years that currently use a peer rate**. A country-year means one country in one year. The full historical low-/middle-income sample contains 1,756 country-years: 970 already use another rate source, 778 use peers and eight have no eligible rate. This experiment changes only the peer fallback; the 970 stronger-source selections remain identical, and the eight previously uncovered observations gain no eligible estimate under these designs. Counts of shadow ratings and counts of usable peer rates are different quantities.

**What was constructed.** A country's growth, income, institutions and public finances determine an approximate credit-strength score. Actual Moody's grades take precedence wherever present; a shadow score fills missing grades for matching. Eligible donor countries then contribute one rate each, in order: primary issuance, IDS terms, secondary rate. The sensitivity adds an existing eligible Moody's-implied rate as the fourth choice. The country being assessed is excluded, at least three distinct donors are required, and the median of their rates is used. Shadow ratings do not generate additional rating-implied donor rates in this run.

**Actual coverage.** “Stop5” means search Rules1–5, then assign no peer. “Strict” means Rules1–3 and5: exclude the rating-alone match as well. Rating proximity is three notches unless expressly identified otherwise.

{coverage_table}
The score-interval design with observed donors retains **638**, an increase of341 over297, and leaves140 without a peer. Adding existing rating-implied donors retains732, an additional94, and leaves46 without a peer. Across the full1,756-country-year sample, total benchmark availability would therefore be1,608 and1,702, respectively, including the970 unchanged stronger-source cases. These totals are arithmetic results of this experiment, not a claim that all resulting benchmarks have the same evidential strength.

**Where those peers come from.** The numbered rule map is unchanged. Each row counts the first eligible rule with at least three countries. This table uses only the original778 targets; no additional targets inflate it.

{table(rule_rows,rule_cols)}
Current labels map in order to `same_income_region_rating3_min3`, `same_income_rating3_min3`, `same_region_rating3_min3`, `rating3_min3`, `same_income_region_min3`, `same_income_min3`, `same_region_min3`, and `global_min3`. Rules1–4 use the actual or conditional shadow rating as described above.

With observed donors, allowing Rule4 adds169 covered country-years compared with the strict interval design:638 versus469. Rule4 is selected for208 observations, including39 that could fall through to Rule5 if Rule4 were excluded. With rating-implied donors added, Rule4 adds34:732 versus698. This is why the matching choice matters separately from the donor-source choice.

**Inputs now available and still missing.** The exact November2018 indicator thresholds, uneven numerical score midpoints, conditional weights and all675 matrix cells are implemented. Historical IMF WEO vintages provide growth, inflation, GDP, PPP income, fiscal balances, debt and debt trends. Previously saved World Bank governance data cover all778 target observations. Newly downloaded World Bank quarterly debt data provide an actual general-government foreign-currency share for56 targets;16 lacked even the earlier external-debt approximation, while40 replace that approximation. The historical IDA/HIPC exception applies to647 targets;531 of those lack gross-interest measures but do not require them because their interest weight is zero.

{table(mi,[('variable','Input or explicitly identified substitute'),('available','Available /778'),('missing','Missing /778')])}
Missing counts overlap and must not be added. The precise country-years are in `missing_inputs_by_country_year.csv`. The foreign-currency numerator and denominator are both from the same general-government Q4 QPSD observation when available. Otherwise, the point scenarios use the World Bank paper's external-PPG-debt fallback, explicitly retained as a proxy. Ratios outside0–100% are not silently capped. Net interest inferred from WEO fiscal balances remains an approximation to the required gross interest; revised GFS gross measures take priority only when both required ratios are present.

**Competitiveness after2017.** “After2027” was interpreted as “after2017,” within the2012–2024 study window. The [WEF2019 dataset](https://www3.weforum.org/docs/WEF_GCI_4.0_2019_Dataset.xlsx) was downloaded and contains412 numeric country observations across2017 backcasts,2018 and2019. Its score is in the `SCORE` row; the apparent `VALUE` row is empty. The new0–100 index is a different construction, not a unit conversion of the old1–7 index. The [WEF2020 report](https://www.weforum.org/publications/the-global-competitiveness-report-2020/in-full/) explicitly pauses comparative rankings; this search did not recover a comparable2020–2023 continuation.

Three treatments were actually run. First, hold the last legacy score, retaining its age; this is a sensitivity and does not establish that competitiveness stayed constant. Second, construct a monotone empirical crosswalk from133 paired2017 countries, leaving each country's own observation out, and use newer observations with a maximum two-year carry. This is a calibration between competitiveness indices, not the previously deferred sovereign-rating regression. Its held-out absolute error is{sum(float(r['absolute_error']) for r in bridge)/len(bridge):.3f} on the old1–7 scale. It modestly extends coverage but does not solve the later publication gap. Third, where a recent legacy score is missing, allow every published competitiveness category and propagate that uncertainty through its12.5% economic-factor weight.

**How score intervals work.** Instead of inventing a precise value for a missing input, calculate the best and worst score allowed by specified input ranges. Missing competitiveness can take any of its15 categories; missing positive-weight interest indicators can take any category; the default adjustment varies0–3 and the currency adjustment0–6, subject to the low-debt cap and combined fiscal-adjustment cap. The peer counts quoted as “score intervals” require rating proximity within three notches for every combination inside these ranges. They do not use the interval centre for matching. Matrix monotonicity and an exhaustive3,375-factor-combination check justify calculating the endpoints.

These are **input-sensitivity ranges, not statistical confidence intervals**. They do not cover all model error, revised-data error, the net-interest approximation, or a different event-risk assumption. They do not guarantee that the actual unobserved rating lies inside. Among854 rated low-/middle-income observations, approximately72.8% of actual grades lie inside the calculated ranges. The median donor rate and earliest matching rule can also differ under other input realizations; what is certified within the stated ranges is the selected donors' rating proximity, not invariance of the entire benchmark.

**What the World Bank reconstruction does and does not replicate.** The quantitative lookup tables are exact. Following [WPS9649](https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/565681620234717531/pdf/Economic-Governance-Improvements-and-Sovereign-Financing-Costs-in-Developing-Countries.pdf), growth/inflation means use seven years and event risk is fixed at moderate; other nonautomated adjustments are neutral. The paper removed governance indicators to avoid overlap with its governance-regression explanatory variable. That reason does not apply to peer matching, so both the inflation-only version and a version restoring Moody's published governance weights were constructed.

Default-event dates from the user's Moody's study were transcribed and checked against Exhibit2. No event after the relevant reference year is used; the March2024 Argentina event is excluded from all January1 study dates. The table's2020 Argentina episode starts inAugust2019 according to its footnote. The central point scenario assigns two factor-score deductions to one documented recent episode and three to multiple episodes; BoC private bond/bank default stocks supplement the rated-only list. This is an explicit approximation, not a recovered proprietary judgment. Absence from the event list is not proof of no default. The interval version varies the penalty instead. The paper does not uniquely specify every default/hedging/adopted-currency judgment, so none of these outputs is labeled an exact reproduction of the authors' observation-level shadow dataset or an official Moody's rating.

The WEO component uses the vintage preceding the benchmark year, but QPSD, GFS, WDI and WGI are later revised snapshots, and Q4 observations need not have been published byJanuary1. This is therefore a retrospective methodological experiment, not a demonstrated real-time forecasting system. The2018 scorecard is also applied retrospectively to earlier study years.

**Accuracy checks.** On the same677 rated low-/middle-income country-years, restoring governance reduces mean absolute rating error from{float(rv['wb_mae']):.2f} to{float(rv['wgi_mae']):.2f} notches, and signed error from{float(rv['wb_bias']):.2f} to{float(rv['wgi_bias']):.2f}. The governance point version is within two notches78.6% of the time. This favors restoring governance for rating construction, even though the inflation-only variant happens to produce a lower peer-rate error on the observed validation cases. Better rating accuracy and better rate accuracy are separate checks.

For rate validation, the target's observed rating is hidden and the entire target country is excluded from its donor pool. The target's primary rate is then the comparison outcome. Errors below are absolute percentage-point errors, not percentages. Each row's original-method comparator uses exactly that row's cases; row-to-row samples can differ.

{table(validation_rows,[('method','Experiment'),('n','Validation country-years'),('new','New MAE, pp'),('old','Original MAE, same cases')])}
Direct same-case comparisons provide the clearest donor-pool result. Adding rating-implied donors without shadows raises error from1.307 to1.603 on144 shared cases. Adding them to the interval/stop5 design raises error from1.174 to1.281 on205 shared cases, despite increasing deployment coverage. Thus the larger pool is useful, but it has a measured quality cost. In the732-case design,701 groups use at least one rating-implied donor and409 have a majority of rating-implied donors; this changes the composition of existing groups as well as adding94 covered observations. Only five primary-rate validation cases are low-income observations; the larger low-income rating comparison is a separate test and cannot resolve that rate-validation limitation. These checks support a promising experiment, not a conclusion that performance transfers unchanged to every previously unrated borrower.

Relaxing rating proximity from three to five notches in the governance point/strict design raises coverage444→488, but raises shared-case primary-rate error1.213→1.293 on194 cases. Keeping income-only and region-only fallbacks through Rule7 raises observed-donor coverage to771, but it returns to the broader matching assumptions the user questioned. The optimistic interval designs are retained in the detailed experiment outputs and are not recommended as deployable results.

**Downstream calculations.** The candidate selection and saved loan cash flows were rerun separately. Of881 existing low-/middle-income CRS records using peers, the observed-donor interval/stop5 design retains773 and loses108; the added-implied-donor design retains830 and loses51. Among256 modern2018–2024 CRS peer records, those figures are249 and256. For retained modern records, mean absolute changes in the calculated grant element are4.48 and5.26 percentage points, respectively. The separate MPG/AidData/ADD outputs are also replayed; their records must not be pooled as independent loans. Any adoption would require recomputing peer-dependent selection, valuation and thesis tables. No production result was changed.

**Recommendation.** Use the governance-restored scorecard with observed primary/IDS/secondary donors as the leading research candidate. Keep the three-notch radius. Carry both the469-case strict result and638-case stop5 result: the latter is defensible only with explicit acceptance of rating-alone matching as a separate weaker category. The732-case version with rating-implied donors is a useful coverage sensitivity, but should not replace the observed-donor result solely because it covers more observations. Indefinite competitiveness carry is a diagnostic; propagating its uncertainty is the cleaner response to the missing continuation. The remaining empirical weaknesses are the fixed event-risk assumption, proxy fiscal inputs and thin primary-rate validation for low-income countries—not missing conversion matrices or a still-unbuilt scorecard.

Source access requiring further manual help is optional for this build. The high-value remaining inputs are country/year-specific general-government foreign-currency debt and gross-interest measures, obtainable from Moody's Country Credit Statistical Handbook or IMF ArticleIV/debt-sustainability reports. A comparable post2019 legacy competitiveness series was not found; university authentication alone would not make a discontinued series exist. Exact source gaps are listed by country-year rather than treated as a generic access block.

New official sources: [World Bank QPSD](https://www.worldbank.org/en/programs/debt-statistics/qpsd), [FY12 IDA eligibility annex, p10](https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/527521468339906853/pdf/879650BR0IDA0S0IC0disclosed05080140.pdf), [FY13 eligibility annex, p39](https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/331101468325187617/pdf/759260BR0IDA0S00Disclosed0308020130.pdf), [IDA graduation history](https://ida.worldbank.org/en/about/borrowing-countries/ida-graduates), [Fiji2019 entry](https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2019/04/26/major-boost-for-fiji-with-additional-world-bank-support), [Sri Lanka December2022 reentry](https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/12/05/world-bank-approves-sri-lanka-s-eligibility-to-access-concessional-financing-to-help-stabilize-the-economy), and [IMF HIPC roster](https://www.imf.org/en/about/factsheets/sheets/2023/debt-relief-under-the-heavily-indebted-poor-countries-initiative-hipc). Direct HTML downloads for some source pages were blocked; their content was read through web search/open. Downloaded data/PDF snapshots, retrieval receipts and hashes are preserved separately.

The build passes45 scorecard checks, the matching boundary tests and16 end-to-end checks, including exact replay of the original778 peer rates/rules/memberships, source priority, target exclusion, unchanged higher tiers, loan-formula replay and production-file hash preservation. No sovereign-rating regression was fitted. All34 peer designs, five scorecard scenarios, per-country diagnostics and source receipts are preserved under the isolated experiment's `constructed_v1` directory; `peer_final` is the current completed run, while `peer` preserves the earlier preliminary run.
'''
